{0 Support and known issues}

Behavior that differs from JavaScript Workers and native OCaml, and features
not supported yet.

{1 Differences from JavaScript Workers}

- A handler waits only through {!Workero.Promise.await}, {!Workero.fetch} and the
  body readers. A function that JavaScript calls, such as a [setTimeout]
  callback, cannot wait. See {{!page-async}Waiting for JavaScript}.
- {!Workero.URL} uses the WHATWG [URL], but {!Workero.URL.path} is decoded and
  only [http] and [https] URLs parse. See
  {{!page-requests}Requests and responses}.
- A response cannot have a status below 200 or above 599. The runtime rejects
  them, so {!Workero.Status} has [`Continue] and [`Switching_Protocols] only to
  read codes; use {!Workero.Websocket} for 101.

{1 Limits of streams, forms and WebSockets}

- A client disconnect shows only as a failed {!Workero.Stream.Writer.write}.
- {!Workero.Response.copy} and {!Workero.Response.of_stream} use up the original
  body. Call {!Workero.Response.clone} first to keep it.
- {!Workero.Request.form_data} holds the whole upload in memory. See
  {{!page-forms}Forms}.

{1 Limits of bindings}

- {{!page-queue}Queues}: a consumer cannot see the content type. {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_json} on a [Text] body gives a JSON string, and the other readers raise [Invalid_argument] on a body of another type. A [v8] body is readable only through {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_js}, and a producer cannot send one.
- Queues: [send] and [send_batch] take only [delay_seconds]. The runtime checks the size limits. Dead letter queues and [max_retries] are set in [wrangler.toml], not in code.
- {{!page-rate_limiter}Rate limiter}: the key is the only argument. The limit and period come from [wrangler.toml]. The count is per location and approximate.
- {{!page-hyperdrive}Hyperdrive}: connection details only. There is no [connect] and no query driver, so workero cannot read from the database yet. The host and password are valid only inside the running Worker. In [workerd] the host is a fake name with no database behind it.
- {{!page-ai}Workers AI}: no streaming answers and no gateway options. {!Workero.Ai.run} raises [Invalid_argument] on a stream.
- {{!page-analytics_engine}Analytics Engine}: [write] returns no result, and the runtime checks the limit of 20 blobs and 20 doubles. There is no query API.

- {{!page-socket}Sockets}: outbound connections only, from a handler. There is no [connect] handler for incoming TCP and no [Socket.upgraded]. {!Workero.Socket.opened} gives no address information. [workerd] cannot test TLS: with a Worker as outbound, [secure_transport:On] and [start_tls] fail. Cloudflare limits the destination (no Cloudflare addresses, loopback or port 25) and the number of open connections for each request; see its documentation.
- Sockets: [index.js] always imports [cloudflare:sockets]. It is available on Cloudflare and in [workerd] without a flag. A program run outside a Worker, for example in Node.js, raises [Failure] from {!Workero.Socket.connect}.
- {{!page-email}Email}: [workerd] has no way to deliver a test email, and it has no [cloudflare:email] module. {!Workero.Email.reply} fails there with a {!Workero.Js_error}. The tests call the handler from a JavaScript harness with a mock message and a stand-in module, so they do not prove what the real runtime accepts.
- Email: {!Workero.Email.reply} takes the raw message as a string, not a stream. {!Workero.Email.raw_text} and {!Workero.Email.raw_bytes} copy the whole message into the memory of the program; use {!Workero.Email.raw} for a large message. The raw message can be read once. The runtime checks the rules for [forward] headers and for the [reply] message.

{1 Limits of entrypoints}

- {{!page-entrypoints}Entrypoints}: arguments and results are JSON values. [Uint8Array], [Date], [Map], [BigInt], streams and stubs do not cross the call. The [scheduled], [queue], [email] and [tail] handlers of a class are not supported.
- The runtime creates a new instance of an entrypoint for each call, so it keeps no state between calls.
- An entrypoint and a Durable Object class cannot share a name.
- A method declared in [~rpc] on a class whose handlers have no [rpc] fails on the first call, not at build time.

{1 Limits of Durable Objects}

- Only classes with SQLite storage work. A class listed in [new_classes] builds, then fails at run time with a JavaScript error. See {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.
- RPC methods on a stub: names are declared with [register ~rpc]. Arguments and results are JSON. [Date], [Map], [BigInt], [Uint8Array], streams, stubs and functions cannot cross the call. A method name cannot be [fetch], [alarm], [connect], [constructor], [prototype] or a [webSocket*] handler.
- Stored values are JSON. A [Date] that other code stores reads back as its ISO string and a [Map] as [{}]. A [BigInt] or a cyclic value raises [Invalid_argument].
- The function given to [register] cannot call {!Workero.Promise.await}. Load stored values in [~init]. Keep per-object state in closures.
- {!Workero.Durable_object.register} must run when the module starts. A [register] call that a condition skips is not exported.
- [workero build] needs [workerd]. The main module must be [index.js].
- A [block_concurrency_while] function that raises resets the object.
- SQL: [Int] values are exact only between -2^53 and 2^53; a larger integer reads back as a [Float]. A whole-number [REAL] such as [2.0] reads back as [Int]. There is no asynchronous transaction. [Sql.run] gives the number of rows written only.
- {{!page-websockets}WebSockets}: a socket from {!Workero.Websocket.accept} does not hibernate. Do not mix it with [accept_websocket]. A class without [websocket_message] accepts hibernatable sockets and drops their messages.
- {{!page-durable_objects}Containers}: {!Workero.Durable_object.Container.get_tcp_port} supports HTTP [fetch] only; raw TCP [connect], [setInactivityTimeout] and outbound interception are not wrapped. The tests run [State.container] against [None] in [workerd] and against a JavaScript stub; a separate test, [test/worker-containers], starts real containers in CI and prints [SKIP containers] where Docker or its images are missing. [workerd] runs containers only with Docker and an egress interceptor image.
- [workerd] cannot test hibernation (eviction and re-creation), jurisdictions, or the tag and 2048-byte attachment limits.

{1 Not supported yet}

- The [tail] handler.
- Workflows.
- Durable Objects without SQLite storage.
- Vectorize and the Send Email binding.
- [HTMLRewriter].
- [AbortController] and request cancellation.
- Most of [crypto.subtle]. {!Workero.Crypto} has digests, HMAC, AES-GCM, PBKDF2
  and HKDF with raw keys. It has no key generation, no [exportKey], no [jwk],
  [pkcs8] or [spki] import, no RSA, ECDSA, ECDH, Ed25519, AES-CBC or AES-CTR, no
  [wrapKey] and no [DigestStream]. Keys are never extractable.

{1 Differences from native OCaml}

- [Sys.word_size] is 32, and [int] has 31 bits.
- There is no file system, except files that you embed. See
  {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries}.
- The program has no threads, sockets or sub-processes.

{1 Report a problem}

Open an issue in the
{{:https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero}workero repository}. Include the
OCaml, wasm_of_ocaml and Wrangler versions, and the smallest worker that shows
the problem.
